The Initial Repository

Suppose your repository starts like this:

A ---- B ---- C  (main)

Now you create a feature branch.

                D ---- E  (feature-login)
               /
A ---- B ---- C  (main)

Meanwhile another developer commits to main.

                D ---- E  (feature-login)
               /
A ---- B ---- C ---- F  (main)

Your branch is now behind main.

Option 1: Git Merge

Command:

git switch main
git merge feature-login

Result:

                D ---- E
               /        \
A ---- B ---- C ---- F ---- M

M is the Merge Commit.

Git preserves the exact history of both branches.

What Happens Internally?

Git combines:

Feature branch commits
Main branch commits

into one new merge commit.

Advantages:

Complete history preserved
Easy to understand branch history

Disadvantages:

More merge commits
History can become cluttered
Fast-Forward Merge

Suppose nobody changed main.

A ---- B ---- C  (main)
                \
                 D ---- E

Run:

git merge feature-login

Git simply moves main forward.

A ---- B ---- C ---- D ---- E

No merge commit is created.

Three-Way Merge

If both branches changed:

                D ---- E
               /
A ---- B ---- C ---- F

Git creates:

                D ---- E
               /        \
A ---- B ---- C ---- F ---- M

This is called a Three-Way Merge because Git compares:

Common ancestor
Main branch
Feature branch
Option 2: Git Rebase

Instead of merging:

git switch feature-login
git rebase main

Git replays your commits on top of the latest main.

Before:

                D ---- E
               /
A ---- B ---- C ---- F

After:

A ---- B ---- C ---- F ---- D' ---- E'

D' and E' are new commits with new commit hashes.

No merge commit.

Why Does Rebase Change Commit IDs?

Each Git commit contains:

Parent commit
Timestamp
Metadata
SHA hash

When Git changes the parent commit during a rebase, it creates a new commit with a new SHA.

Merge vs Rebase
Merge History
                D ---- E
               /        \
A ---- B ---- C ---- F ---- M

Complete branch history.

Rebase History
A ---- B ---- C ---- F ---- D' ---- E'

Linear history.

Comparison
Merge	Rebase
Preserves branch history	Creates linear history
Merge commit may be created	No merge commit
Safer for shared branches	Best before sharing or on local branches
Easier to audit	Cleaner commit history
Interactive Rebase

Command:

git rebase -i HEAD~3

Git opens an editor:

pick a1 First Commit
pick b2 Second Commit
pick c3 Third Commit

You can:

Reorder commits
Edit commit messages
Squash commits
Drop commits
Squash Commits

Suppose you have:

Added Login

Fixed Login

Updated Login

Removed Debug

Instead of four commits:

A
B
C
D

Use interactive rebase:

git rebase -i HEAD~4

Change:

pick A
squash B
squash C
squash D

Result:

Single Commit

"Implemented Login Feature"

This produces a much cleaner history.

Rebase Conflict

Conflicts can also happen during a rebase.

Git pauses:

CONFLICT

Steps:

Resolve the file.

Stage it:

git add app.py

Continue:

git rebase --continue

Abort if needed:

git rebase --abort
Enterprise Example

Developer creates:

feature-payment

Commits:

Commit 1

Commit 2

Commit 3

Before opening the Pull Request:

git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main

Resolve conflicts.

Push:

git push --force-with-lease

Why --force-with-lease instead of --force?

After a rebase, commit history changes, so a force push is required. --force-with-lease is safer because it refuses to overwrite remote changes made by others.

Merge in GitHub

Most repositories use Pull Requests.

Options include:

Create a merge commit
Squash and merge
Rebase and merge

Each organization chooses a strategy based on its workflow.

Which Should You Use?
Scenario	Recommended
Shared branch	Merge
Local feature branch before PR	Rebase
Clean history	Rebase
Preserve history	Merge
